{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Isn't It a Pity" is a song by English musician George Harrison from his 1970 solo album "All Things Must Pass".","title":"Isn't It a Pity"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It appears in two variations there: one the well-known, seven-minute version; the other a reprise, titled "Isn't It a Pity (Version Two)".","title":"Isn't It a Pity"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Harrison wrote the song in 1966, but it was rejected for inclusion on releases by the Beatles.","title":"Isn't It a Pity"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In many countries around the world, the song was also issued on a double A-side single with "My Sweet Lord".","title":"Isn't It a Pity"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In America, "Billboard" magazine listed it with "My Sweet Lord" when the single topped the Hot 100 chart, while in Canada, "Isn't It a Pity" reached number 1 as the preferred side.","title":"Isn't It a Pity"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"All Things Must Pass is a triple album by English musician George Harrison.","title":"All Things Must Pass"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Recorded and released in 1970, the album was Harrison's first solo work since the break-up of the Beatles in April that year, and his third solo album overall.","title":"All Things Must Pass"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It includes the hit singles "My Sweet Lord" and "What Is Life", as well as songs such as "Isn't It a Pity" and the title track that had been turned down for inclusion on releases by the Beatles.","title":"All Things Must Pass"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The album reflects the influence of Harrison's musical activities with artists such as Bob Dylan, the Band, Delaney & Bonnie and Billy Preston during 1968–70, and his growth as an artist beyond his supporting role to former bandmates John Lennon and Paul McCartney.","title":"All Things Must Pass"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" "All Things Must Pass" introduced Harrison's signature sound, the slide guitar, and the spiritual themes that would be present throughout his subsequent solo work.","title":"All Things Must Pass"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The original vinyl release consisted of two LPs of songs and a third disc of informal jams, titled "Apple Jam".","title":"All Things Must Pass"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Several commentators interpret Barry Feinstein's album cover photo, showing Harrison surrounded by four garden gnomes, as a statement on his independence from the Beatles.","title":"All Things Must Pass"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""I Live for You" is a song by English musician George Harrison originally recorded during the sessions for his "All Things Must Pass" triple album in 1970.","title":"I Live for You"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Long available on bootlegs, the song was finally released officially as a bonus track on the 30th anniversary reissue of "All Things Must Pass" in January 2001.","title":"I Live for You"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The released recording features only Harrison's lead vocal and Pete Drake's prominent pedal-steel guitar from the 1970 album sessions, with all other instruments overdubbed by Harrison and his son Dhani in 2000.","title":"I Live for You"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Despite the wealth of unreleased material recorded for "All Things Must Pass", it was the only new song included with the album's 2001 reissue.","title":"I Live for You"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Music critics recognise "I Live for You" as one of many George Harrison compositions that can be interpreted as both a traditional love song and a devotional song.","title":"I Live for You"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Run of the Mill" is a song by English musician George Harrison, released on his 1970 triple album "All Things Must Pass".","title":"Run of the Mill (George Harrison song)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Harrison wrote the song shortly after the Beatles' troubled "Get Back" sessions in early 1969, during a period when his growth as a songwriter had inadvertently contributed to the dysfunction within the Beatles' group dynamic.","title":"Run of the Mill (George Harrison song)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The lyrics reflect the toll that running their company Apple Corps had taken on relationships within the band, especially between Paul McCartney and the other three Beatles, as well as Harrison's dismay at John Lennon's emotional withdrawal from the band.","title":"Run of the Mill (George Harrison song)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Commentators recognise "Run of the Mill" as one of several Harrison compositions that provide an insight into events behind the Beatles' break-up, particularly the difficulties surrounding Apple.","title":"Run of the Mill (George Harrison song)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""All Things Must Pass" is a song by English musician George Harrison, issued in November 1970 as the title track to his triple album of the same name.","title":"All Things Must Pass (song)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Billy Preston released the song originally – as "All Things (Must) Pass" – on his Apple Records album "Encouraging Words" (1970), after the Beatles had rejected it for inclusion on their "Let It Be" album in January 1969.","title":"All Things Must Pass (song)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The composition reflects the influence of the Band's sound and communal music-making on Harrison, after he had spent time with the group in Woodstock, New York, in late 1968, while Timothy Leary's poem "All Things Pass", a psychedelic adaptation of the Tao Te Ching, provided inspiration for his song lyrics.","title":"All Things Must Pass (song)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Behind That Locked Door" is a song by English musician George Harrison, released on his 1970 triple album "All Things Must Pass".","title":"Behind That Locked Door"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Harrison wrote the song in August 1969 as a message of encouragement to Bob Dylan, who was making a highly publicised comeback to the concert stage, accompanied by the Band, with a headlining performance at the Isle of Wight Festival.","title":"Behind That Locked Door"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" "Behind That Locked Door" is a rare Harrison composition in the country music genre and the second song dealing with the friendship between himself and Dylan, after their 1968 collaboration "I'd Have You Anytime".","title":"Behind That Locked Door"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Its lyrics address Dylan's elusive nature, and reflect the high regard in which Harrison held the American singer's work.","title":"Behind That Locked Door"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The same reluctance on Dylan's part to re-engage with a concert audience led to him retreating again from live performance until August 1971, when he responded to Harrison's request to play at the Concert for Bangladesh.","title":"Behind That Locked Door"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: seven minutes
